            {
              "id": "b53d13c9-406a-338c-9c54-432398f9eab8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions in a C++ program. In the provided code snippet, the SHA-1 hash algorithm is being used, which is considered insecure for many cryptographic functions as it is susceptible to collision attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger hash algorithm such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These algorithms are currently considered secure and are not known to have any significant vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of SHA-1 with a stronger hash algorithm. The specific fix would depend on the rest of your code, but the line in question would change from:\n\n```cpp\nheader->HashAlgorithm == uint16_t(GlobalTypeHashAlg::SHA1_8)\n```\n\nto something like:\n\n```cpp\nheader->HashAlgorithm == uint16_t(GlobalTypeHashAlg::SHA256_8)\n```\n\nor\n\n```cpp\nheader->HashAlgorithm == uint16_t(GlobalTypeHashAlg::SHA3_8)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe specific library dependencies would depend on the rest of your code. However, you would likely need a library that provides the SHA-256 or SHA-3 hash functions. One such library is OpenSSL.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "03dd26cc-2710-3238-98d7-c7f8cff74c2e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions during the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may alias each other, as suggested by the observed assignment pattern. The verdict for this call site is also marked as genuine. The source and destination are both pointers, and there is no evidence of explicit overlap prevention. Although the source and destination are not null at this point, the main risk is the potential for overlapping memory, which can cause undefined behavior with memcpy. No additional safety checks or guards are present to mitigate this risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the use of `memcpy` with `memmove`, which is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. This change ensures that the copy operation will not result in data corruption or unpredictable behavior if the source and destination overlap.\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(sym, d, sizeof(DefinedRegular));\n```\n\n## References\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
