            {
              "id": "14a97ec5-b074-3766-8d9e-2c07f422c1c3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions in a software application. In C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a developer uses a hash function that is known to be vulnerable to collision attacks, such as SHA-1.\n\nSHA-1 is a cryptographic hash function that produces a 160-bit (20-byte) hash value. It is commonly used in various security applications and protocols. However, SHA-1 is not considered secure against well-funded attackers. In particular, SHA-1 is vulnerable to collision attacks, where two different inputs produce the same hash output. This can lead to various security issues, such as data integrity violations and unauthorized information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should avoid using SHA-1 and instead use stronger hash functions, such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered secure and are not known to be vulnerable to collision attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you are using the OpenSSL library to compute SHA-1 hashes, you can replace the `SHA1` function with the `SHA256` function to compute SHA-256 hashes. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\nvoid compute_hash(const unsigned char* data, size_t len, unsigned char* md) {\n    SHA256(data, len, md);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the OpenSSL library. You can install it on Ubuntu with the following command:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get install libssl-dev\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "45ce9bbc-33a1-362e-b13d-2c04717817c6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The code copies data from one buffer to another using strncpy, but dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions, which is not allowed by the C standard for strncpy and can result in unpredictable behavior. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of a guard for buffer sizes or null checks further increases the risk. These factors indicate a real and actionable issue that should be addressed to prevent undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the strncpy operation, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use a memory-safe function such as memmove, which is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, add explicit null pointer checks for both the source and destination to prevent null pointer dereference. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (hdr && name.data() && (hdr->Name != name.data())) {\n    // Safe to use strncpy if no overlap\n    strncpy(hdr->Name, name.data(), std::min(name.size(), (size_t)COFF::NameSize));\n} else if (hdr && name.data()) {\n    // Use memmove if overlap is possible\n    memmove(hdr->Name, name.data(), std::min(name.size(), (size_t)COFF::NameSize));\n}\n```\n\nThis approach checks for null pointers and uses memmove only if the source and destination may overlap, otherwise it uses strncpy as intended. Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the copied data to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1f905b8b-4462-3454-bfee-e3a4a6332531",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions in a software application. In C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a developer uses a hash function that is known to be vulnerable to collision attacks, such as SHA-1.\n\nSHA-1 is a cryptographic hash function that produces a 160-bit (20-byte) hash value. It is commonly used in various security applications and protocols. However, SHA-1 is not considered secure against well-funded attackers. In particular, SHA-1 is vulnerable to collision attacks, where two different inputs produce the same hash output. This can lead to various security issues, such as data integrity violations and unauthorized information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should avoid using SHA-1 and instead use stronger hash functions, such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered secure and are not known to be vulnerable to collision attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you are using the OpenSSL library to compute SHA-1 hashes, you can replace the `SHA1` function with the `SHA256` function to compute SHA-256 hashes. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\nvoid compute_hash(const unsigned char* data, size_t len, unsigned char* md) {\n    SHA256(data, len, md);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the OpenSSL library. You can install it on Ubuntu with the following command:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get install libssl-dev\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
