            {
              "id": "20a9f6aa-42df-3a70-95f0-fcc42f41a743",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions in a software application. In C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a developer uses a hash function that is known to be vulnerable to collision attacks, such as SHA-1.\n\nSHA-1 is a cryptographic hash function that produces a 160-bit (20-byte) hash value. It is commonly used in various security applications and protocols. However, SHA-1 is not considered secure against well-funded attackers. In particular, SHA-1 is vulnerable to collision attacks, where two different inputs produce the same hash output. This can lead to various security issues, such as data integrity violations and unauthorized information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should avoid using SHA-1 and instead use stronger hash functions, such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered secure and are not known to be vulnerable to collision attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you are using SHA-1 in your C++ code, you can replace it with SHA-256 using the OpenSSL library. Here is an example of how to do this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\nvoid sha256_hash_string(unsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH], char outputBuffer[65])\n{\n    int i = 0;\n\n    for(i = 0; i < SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++)\n    {\n        sprintf(outputBuffer + (i * 2), \"%02x\", hash[i]);\n    }\n\n    outputBuffer[64] = 0;\n}\n\nvoid sha256_string(char *string, char outputBuffer[65])\n{\n    unsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\n\n    SHA256_CTX sha256;\n    SHA256_Init(&sha256);\n    SHA256_Update(&sha256, string, strlen(string));\n    SHA256_Final(hash, &sha256);\n\n    sha256_hash_string(hash, outputBuffer);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code requires the OpenSSL library. You can install it on Ubuntu with the following command:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get install libssl-dev\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858"
                ]
              }
