            {
              "id": "33f7d877-71fe-367a-a15c-db4d0c627306",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions in a software application. In C++, this vulnerability can occur when a developer uses a weak hash function like MD5. MD5 is considered weak because it is susceptible to collision attacks, where two different inputs produce the same hash output, thereby compromising the integrity of the data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use stronger hash functions like SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered secure and are not susceptible to known collision attacks. It's also important to keep up-to-date with the latest cryptographic research to ensure that the hash functions used in your application remain secure.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of MD5 with a stronger hash function. Here's an example of how to use SHA-256 with the OpenSSL library in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string sha256(const std::string str)\n{\n    unsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\n    SHA256_CTX sha256;\n    SHA256_Init(&sha256);\n    SHA256_Update(&sha256, str.c_str(), str.size());\n    SHA256_Final(hash, &sha256);\n    std::stringstream ss;\n    for(int i = 0; i < SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++)\n    {\n        ss << std::hex << std::setw(2) << std::setfill('0') << (int)hash[i];\n    }\n    return ss.str();\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the OpenSSL library. You can install it on Ubuntu with the following command:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get install libssl-dev\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
