            {
              "id": "5b9f571e-7d55-3033-bdf7-41373fc41e4f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions in a software application. In the context of C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when hash functions like SHA-1 are used, which are no longer considered secure against well-funded attackers. \n\nIn the provided code sink `s == \"sha1\" || s == \"tree\"`, the application is checking if the hash algorithm being used is SHA-1, which is a vulnerable hash algorithm.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use stronger hash functions like SHA-256 or SHA-3. It is also important to keep up-to-date with the latest cryptographic standards and recommendations, as the strength of hash functions can change over time as new attacks are discovered.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of SHA-1 with a stronger hash function. The specific fix would depend on the rest of your code, but in general, you would replace instances of SHA-1 with SHA-256 or SHA-3. For example:\n\n```cpp\n// Old code\nif (s == \"sha1\" || s == \"tree\") {\n    // ...\n}\n\n// New code\nif (s == \"sha256\" || s == \"sha3\") {\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe specific library dependencies would depend on the rest of your code. However, to use SHA-256 or SHA-3 in C++, you might need libraries such as:\n\n- OpenSSL\n- Crypto++\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Insecure Cryptographic Storage](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
