            {
              "id": "0ed592e5-f724-3a1b-8ed5-21db802818a8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string up to, but not including, the null character. If the string does not have a null character, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters one, which can lead to buffer overflows and other memory-related vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(\"level=\")` is used to get the length of the string \"level=\". This is not a vulnerability in itself, but it can become one if the `strlen` function is used improperly elsewhere in the code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Avoid using `strlen` on user-supplied input where possible, as this can lead to buffer overflows if the input is not null-terminated. Instead, consider using safer alternatives such as `strnlen`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read past this length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code snippet does not contain a vulnerability, as the string \"level=\" is a string literal and is guaranteed to be null-terminated. However, if `arg` is a C-style string and not a `std::string`, it should be converted to a `std::string` before calling `substr` to avoid potential issues with null-termination.\n\n```cpp\nstd::string arg_str(arg);\narg_str = arg_str.substr(strlen(\"level=\"));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::string` and its `substr` method.\n- `<cstring>`: for `strlen`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
